From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: A733 - give the HS400 data-strobe delay an actual value

host->ds_dl was never assigned. It came out of kzalloc as 0 and went straight
into SMHC_DS_DL on every HS400 mode switch, so the strobe delay this driver has
been running is not a chosen value, it is an unset one.

That matters more than a number usually would, because the data strobe is what
frames HS400 data. A delay outside the eye does not merely skew the bits, it
loses the start of the block -- which is exactly the error class the board
reports with the HS400 2x timing mode applied and everything else matching the vendor:

    876 x  sunxi-mmc 4022000.mmc: smc 1 err, cmd 18, RD DCE SBE !!
    dd: /dev/mmcblk1: Input/output error   (0 bytes transferred)

A live sweep on the 8 GiB board read data for every delay from 2 to 40 and nothing at 0 or
from 48 upward. Take that as a candidate eye, not as proof: the sweep poked the
register between transfers, and the SMHC2 aperture only answers while the module
clock runs, so some of those writes may never have reached the hardware (on a
fresh boot the same register reads 0x00000000 although the driver had written
0x80). What the sweep cannot be wrong about is the endpoint it started from: 0
delivered nothing, and 0 is what we were running.

So the value now comes from the cfg, as a controller/board property, and travels
through sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable() with the card clock gated like every other
timing value -- rather than being poked in from userspace, which is how the last
two measurement rounds went wrong. 20 sits in the middle of the candidate eye
rather than on an edge. The vendor runs 2, but arrives there through a runtime
retry recalibration, not as a constant, so its value is not transferable.

This is a stopgap with a mechanism, not a tuning solution: the honest fix is an
->execute_hs400_tuning callback that sweeps the delay against an EXT_CSD read at
init, the way the vendor recalibrates and the way mtk-sd does it. `mmc-sunxi-add-a733-emmc-hs400` once
carried such a sweep and it was dropped. Re-introducing it is the follow-up; this
patch makes the default stop being an accident in the meantime.

Test that decides it: cold boot, then a 64 MiB read twice with md5 compared, on
the first access after init -- not after any failed transfer, because a bad delay
leaves the controller wedged until a power cycle and every later measurement is
then worthless.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -296,12 +296,15 @@ struct sunxi_mmc_cfg {
 
 	/* controller carries the HS400 data-strobe delay path */
 	bool can_hs400;
 
 	/* SMHC_FIFOTH value; 0 selects SDXC_FIFOTH_DEFAULT */
 	u32 fifoth;
+
+	/* HS400 data-strobe delay line value (0..63) */
+	u32 ds_dl;
 };
 
 struct sunxi_mmc_host {
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct mmc_host	*mmc;
 	struct reset_control *reset;
@@ -907,15 +910,23 @@ static void sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
 	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_SD_NTSR);
 	rval &= ~(SDXC_NTSR_DAT_SAM_PH_MASK | SDXC_HS400_NEW_SAM_EN);
 	rval |= SDXC_NTSR_MODE_SEL;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_SD_NTSR, rval);
 
 	/*
-	 * Data-strobe delay: enable the software delay line, load
-	 * host->ds_dl. Stays 0: at 150 MHz every delay reads back, the
-	 * sweep was dropped, and the UM prescribes no value.
+	 * Data-strobe delay: enable the software delay line and load the
+	 * delay. This is where HS400 decides whether it can find the start of
+	 * a block at all -- the strobe is what frames the data, and a delay
+	 * outside the eye produces start-bit errors rather than merely skewed
+	 * data (measured 2026-07-25: 876 x "cmd 18, RD DCE SBE" with the delay
+	 * at 0, and the transfer delivering nothing).
+	 *
+	 * host->ds_dl was never assigned, so it was 0 from kzalloc -- not a
+	 * chosen value, an unset one. The UM prescribes none, so it comes from
+	 * the cfg and is a board/controller property until a proper
+	 * ->execute_hs400_tuning sweep replaces it.
 	 */
 	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_DS_DL_REG);
 	rval &= ~SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK;
 	rval |= host->ds_dl & SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK;
 	rval |= SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_DS_DL_REG, rval);
@@ -1488,12 +1499,25 @@ static const struct sunxi_mmc_cfg sun60i_a733_emmc_cfg = {
 	/* UM 11.3.8.17 recommends BSIZE_OF_TRANS = 3 (MSize 16), TX_TL = 240
 	 * and RX_TL = 15, and 11.3.6.2 writes 0x300F00F0 verbatim.
 	 * Vendor silicon on the 8 GiB board reads exactly this while writing this eMMC
 	 * at HS400.
 	 */
 	.fifoth = 0x300f00f0,
+	/*
+	 * Data-strobe delay. A live sweep on the 8 GiB board (2026-07-25) read data for
+	 * every delay from 2 to 40 and nothing at 0 or from 48 up, so 20 sits
+	 * in the middle of the candidate eye rather than on its edge. That
+	 * sweep was taken by poking the register between transfers and is NOT
+	 * conclusive -- the aperture only answers while the module clock runs,
+	 * so some of those writes may never have landed. What is certain is
+	 * that the previous value was not chosen at all: host->ds_dl was never
+	 * assigned and came out of kzalloc as 0, the one low value the sweep
+	 * never saw deliver a byte. The vendor runs 2, arrived at by a runtime
+	 * retry recalibration rather than as a constant.
+	 */
+	.ds_dl = 20,
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id sunxi_mmc_of_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun4i-a10-mmc", .data = &sun4i_a10_cfg },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun5i-a13-mmc", .data = &sun5i_a13_cfg },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun7i-a20-mmc", .data = &sun7i_a20_cfg },
@@ -1748,12 +1772,14 @@ static int sunxi_mmc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	 * hardware support on this controller and is never offered.
 	 */
 	if (!host->cfg->can_hs400)
 		mmc->caps2 &= ~MMC_CAP2_HS400;
 	mmc->caps2 &= ~MMC_CAP2_HS400_ES;
 
+	host->ds_dl = host->cfg->ds_dl;
+
 	ret = sunxi_mmc_init_host(host);
 	if (ret)
 		goto error_free_dma;
 
 	pm_runtime_set_active(&pdev->dev);
 	pm_runtime_set_autosuspend_delay(&pdev->dev, 50);
